From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:25:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] migration/qemu-file: inline the in-buffer case of the byte
 reader

Reading one byte cost three out-of-line calls (qemu_get_byte, qemu_peek_byte,
qemu_file_skip) and two asserts, for a bounds check and an array index. A
migration stream barely notices: its bulk goes through qemu_get_buffer. A
device vmstate built from scalar fields is the opposite case, and loading
one is most of a panda_fast_restore once RAM is copied by the dirty set.

Handle the common case, the byte already in the buffer, in a static inline
in this file, used by qemu_get_byte and the be16/be32 readers; refill, EOF
and the asserts stay in the out-of-line functions. A writable file has
buf_size 0, so it cannot take the fast path and still reaches the assert.
struct QEMUFile stays private to this file.

From rehosting/qemu_builder workspace/fastsnap2 (5170d14), patch 0017.

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
Origin: penguin
---
 migration/qemu-file.c | 53 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 43 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/migration/qemu-file.c b/migration/qemu-file.c
index f30e13c66d778b150cc79f9a882053accc9dbb2e..1e4f8ddcce06392cc90ced1651373d4aa1a44753 100644
--- a/migration/qemu-file.c
+++ b/migration/qemu-file.c
@@ -754,13 +754,46 @@ int coroutine_mixed_fn qemu_peek_byte(QEMUFile *f, int offset)
     return f->buf[index];
 }
 
-int coroutine_mixed_fn qemu_get_byte(QEMUFile *f)
+/*
+ * The hot path of every vmstate load.
+ *
+ * Reading one byte went through three out-of-line calls -- qemu_get_byte(),
+ * qemu_peek_byte() and qemu_file_skip() -- plus two asserts, for what is
+ * ultimately a bounds check and an array index. That is affordable on a
+ * migration stream, which reads most of its bulk through qemu_get_buffer().
+ * It is not affordable for device vmstates built from scalar fields: a device
+ * carrying a 1020-element struct array of single-byte fields pays it several
+ * thousand times, and a measured device-state load of 63 KB spent ~40% of its
+ * instructions here, at roughly 92 instructions per byte.
+ *
+ * The common case is that the next byte is already in the buffer, so handle
+ * that inline and leave everything else -- refill, exhaustion, the asserts --
+ * to the out-of-line functions, whose behaviour is unchanged. struct QEMUFile
+ * stays private to this file; only callers within it are inlined, which is
+ * where the scalar readers below live.
+ */
+static inline int qemu_get_byte_fast(QEMUFile *f)
 {
-    int result;
+    int index = f->buf_index;
 
-    result = qemu_peek_byte(f, 0);
+    if (likely(index < f->buf_size)) {
+        /* qemu_file_skip(f, 1) reduces to this: index < buf_size implies
+         * index + 1 <= buf_size, so the skip always advances. */
+        f->buf_index = index + 1;
+        return f->buf[index];
+    }
+
+    /* Buffer empty, at EOF, or writable -- all handled by the slow path,
+     * including the assertions, which cannot be reached on the fast path
+     * without buf_size being non-zero and the file therefore readable. */
+    index = qemu_peek_byte(f, 0);
     qemu_file_skip(f, 1);
-    return result;
+    return index;
+}
+
+int coroutine_mixed_fn qemu_get_byte(QEMUFile *f)
+{
+    return qemu_get_byte_fast(f);
 }
 
 uint64_t qemu_file_transferred(QEMUFile *f)
@@ -800,18 +833,18 @@ void qemu_put_be64(QEMUFile *f, uint64_t v)
 unsigned int qemu_get_be16(QEMUFile *f)
 {
     unsigned int v;
-    v = qemu_get_byte(f) << 8;
-    v |= qemu_get_byte(f);
+    v = qemu_get_byte_fast(f) << 8;
+    v |= qemu_get_byte_fast(f);
     return v;
 }
 
 unsigned int qemu_get_be32(QEMUFile *f)
 {
     unsigned int v;
-    v = (unsigned int)qemu_get_byte(f) << 24;
-    v |= qemu_get_byte(f) << 16;
-    v |= qemu_get_byte(f) << 8;
-    v |= qemu_get_byte(f);
+    v = (unsigned int)qemu_get_byte_fast(f) << 24;
+    v |= qemu_get_byte_fast(f) << 16;
+    v |= qemu_get_byte_fast(f) << 8;
+    v |= qemu_get_byte_fast(f);
     return v;
 }
 
